    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chichen Itza became the dominant capital of the northern Maya lowlands as the southern centres declined, controlling trade through its port of Isla Cerritos.</li><li>Its mix of Maya and central Mexican styles was once read as migration or conquest from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, but most scholars now see it as cultural diffusion.</li><li>The city declined as a regional centre by 1100, yet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote">Sacred Cenote</a> remained a place of pilgrimage when the Spanish arrived.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A handclap before the staircase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-castillo-chichen-itza">Temple of Kukulcán</a> produces an echo that resembles the chirp of a bird, similar to that of the quetzal.</p></aside>
    </section>

